pandas/pull/64864.diff', 'patch_url': 'https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/64864.patch', 'merged_at': None}","## Summary

- **`diff_2d`**: Use raw C pointers for inner loops instead of Cython memoryview indexing (which generates strided pointer arithmetic). This lets the C compiler auto-vectorize the subtraction loop. **~20-25% faster** for `DataFrame.diff()`.
- **`take_2d_axis0`/`axis1`**: Use raw C pointers for inner row copy/conversion loops when rows are contiguous, enabling auto-vectorization. **~10% faster** for cross-type `DataFrame.reindex()`.
- **`nancorr`**: Add `no_nans` fast path that skips per-element mask checks when all data is finite (mirrors the existing pattern in `nancorr_spearman`).
- **`nancorr_spearman`**, **`rank_sorted_1d`**: Add `@cython.cdivision(True)` to remove zero-division checks from hot inner loops.
- **`rank_1d`**: Skip `get_rank_nan_fill_val` + `np.putmask` when `check_mask` is False (integer types without NAs).
- **`pad`/`backfill`/`pad_inplace`/`pad_2d_inplace`**: Release GIL for numeric types via `with nogil(numeric_object_t is not object)`.
- **`get_fill_indexer`**: Release GIL around the fill loop.
- **`unique_deltas`**: Use `malloc`'d C array + `memcpy` instead of Python list to avoid per-element GIL interaction.
- **`is_monotonic`**: Remove redundant assignments before `break`.

## Benchmarks

Alternating runs (branch vs main) with fixed seeds, `min(repeat=5)`:

| Benchmark | Branch (ms) | Main (ms) | Speedup |
|---|---|---|---|
| `DataFrame.diff()` 10k×100 | 0.49 | 0.61 | ~20% |
| `DataFrame.diff()` 100k×10 | 0.46 | 0.61 | ~25% |
| `DataFrame.reindex()` cross-type 10k×50 | 0.31 | 0.34 | ~10% |

## SIMD detail

The key insight is that Cython's memoryview indexing (`arr[i, j]`) generates C code like:
```c
*(type*)(arr.data + i * arr.strides[0] + j * arr.strides[1])
```
Even when we've verified contiguity, the runtime stride multiplications prevent the C compiler from recognizing unit-stride access patterns for auto-vectorization. Using raw typed pointers (`ptr[j]`) gives the compiler the clean `ptr + j * sizeof(type)` pattern it needs.

